# Description

Adds namespaced (by features: `pinocchio`, `solana_program`, and `client`) generated code for 

The code generated from this facilitates automatic APIs for `pinocchio`-based, `solana_sdk`-based, and `client`-based code to call a program's instructions. It namespaces each of the three into nested submodules named `generated_[feature]`. This makes it easy to test multiple features without namespace collisions, or to have a `client` in the same workspace as the program it calls, for example.

To better explain, take this example struct:

```rust
#[repr(u8)]
#[derive(ProgramInstruction)]
#[program_id(crate::program::ID)]
pub enum InstructionTag {
    #[account(0, signer, name = "user", desc = "The user closing their seat.")]
    #[account(1, writable, name = "market_account", desc = "The market account PDA.")]
    #[account( 2, writable, name = "base_user_ata", desc = "The user's associated base mint token account.")]
    #[account( 3, writable, name = "quote_user_ata", desc = "The user's associated quote mint token account.")]
    #[account( 4, writable, name = "base_market_ata", desc = "The market's associated base mint token account.")]
    #[account( 5, writable, name = "quote_market_ata", desc = "The market's associated quote mint token account.")]
    #[account(6, name = "base_mint", desc = "The base token mint account.")]
    #[account(7, name = "quote_mint", desc = "The quote token mint account.")]
    #[account( 8, name = "base_token_program", desc = "The base mint's token program.")]
    #[account( 9, name = "quote_token_program", desc = "The quote mint's token program.")]
    #[args(sector_index_hint: u32, "A hint indicating which sector the user's seat resides in.")]
    #[args(random_variable: u64)]
    #[args(huge_number: u128)]
    #[args(one_byte: u8)]
    CloseSeat,
```

This struct generates the code below.

# The namespaced module

All of the following code (after this) is wrapped in a feature-based generated submodule.

Note that all of the feature-gated code is generated, but each feature-gated submodule will only show up in `cargo expand` if the feature is enabled.

```rust
#[cfg(feature = "pinocchio")]
pub mod generated_pinocchio {
    // ...
}
```

# The instruction data

Code to pack and unpack each instruction variant's data.

### `CloseSeatInstructionData`

```rust
/// `InstructionTag::CloseSeat` instruction data.
///
/// - `sector_index_hint` — A hint indicating which sector the user's seat resides in.
/// - `random_variable`
/// - `huge_number`
/// - `one_byte`
pub struct CloseSeatInstructionData {
    /// A hint indicating which sector the user's seat resides in.
    pub sector_index_hint: u32,
    pub random_variable: u64,
    pub huge_number: u128,
    pub one_byte: u8,
}
```

### The compile time assertion to ensure soundness

```rust
/// Compile time assertion that the size with the tag == the sum of the field sizes.
const _: [(); 30] = [(); 1 + 4 + 8 + 16 + 1];
```

### `new`, `pack`, and `unpack`

`new` is just a helper to avoid multi-line constructors for single-field structs.

`pack` and `unpack` are explained in more detail below.

```rust
impl CloseSeatInstructionData {
    /// `InstructionTag::CloseSeat` instruction data.
    ///
    /// - `sector_index_hint` — A hint indicating which sector the user's seat resides in.
    /// - `random_variable`
    /// - `huge_number`
    /// - `one_byte`
    #[inline(always)]
    pub fn new(
        sector_index_hint: u32,
        random_variable: u64,
        huge_number: u128,
        one_byte: u8,
    ) -> Self {
        Self {
            sector_index_hint,
            random_variable,
            huge_number,
            one_byte,
        }
    }

    /// Instruction data layout:
    /// - [0]: the discriminant `InstructionTag::CloseSeat` (u8, 1 byte)
    /// - [1..5]: the `sector_index_hint` (u32, 4 bytes)
    /// - [5..13]: the `random_variable` (u64, 8 bytes)
    /// - [13..29]: the `huge_number` (u128, 16 bytes)
    /// - [29]: the `one_byte` (u8, 1 byte)
    #[inline(always)]
    pub fn pack(&self) -> [u8; 30] {
        let mut data: [core::mem::MaybeUninit<u8>; 30] = [core::mem::MaybeUninit::uninit(); 30];
        data[0].write(super::InstructionTag::CloseSeat as u8);
        unsafe {
            core::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(
                (&self.sector_index_hint.to_le_bytes()).as_ptr(),
                (&mut data[1..5]).as_mut_ptr() as *mut u8,
                4,
            );
            core::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(
                (&self.random_variable.to_le_bytes()).as_ptr(),
                (&mut data[5..13]).as_mut_ptr() as *mut u8,
                8,
            );
            core::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(
                (&self.huge_number.to_le_bytes()).as_ptr(),
                (&mut data[13..29]).as_mut_ptr() as *mut u8,
                16,
            );
            core::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(
                (&self.one_byte.to_le_bytes()).as_ptr(),
                (&mut data[29..30]).as_mut_ptr() as *mut u8,
                1,
            );
        }
        unsafe { *(data.as_ptr() as *const [u8; 30]) }
    }
    
    /// This method unpacks the instruction data that comes *after* the discriminant has
    /// already been peeled off of the front of the slice.
    /// Trailing bytes are ignored; the length must be sufficient, not exact.
    #[inline(always)]
    pub fn unpack(
        instruction_data: &[u8],
    ) -> Result<Self, pinocchio::program_error::ProgramError> {
        if instruction_data.len() < 29 {
            return Err(
                pinocchio::program_error::ProgramError::InvalidInstructionData,
            );
        }
        unsafe {
            let p = instruction_data.as_ptr();
            let sector_index_hint = u32::from_le_bytes(*(p as *const [u8; 4]));
            let random_variable = u64::from_le_bytes(
                *(p.add(4) as *const [u8; 8]),
            );
            let huge_number = u128::from_le_bytes(
                *(p.add(12) as *const [u8; 16]),
            );
            let one_byte = u8::from_le_bytes(*(p.add(28) as *const [u8; 1]));
            Ok(Self {
                sector_index_hint,
                random_variable,
                huge_number,
                one_byte,
            })
        }
    }
}
```

# The instruction accounts context and invoke functions

The `CloseSeat` struct facilitates invoking the instruction from an external program, or 
creating the instruction in a client (the client code has different code than the `pinocchio` code below).

### The accounts struct

This is essentially the instruction's account "context".

```rust
/// The invocation struct for a `InstructionTag::CloseSeat` instruction.
///
/// # Caller Guarantees
///
/// When invoking this instruction as a cross-program invocation, caller must ensure that:
/// - WRITE accounts are not currently borrowed in *any* capacity.
/// - READ accounts are not currently mutably borrowed.
///
/// ### Accounts
/// 0. `[READ, SIGNER]` `user` The user closing their seat.
/// 1. `[WRITE]` `market_account` The market account PDA.
/// 2. `[WRITE]` `base_user_ata` The user's associated base mint token account.
/// 3. `[WRITE]` `quote_user_ata` The user's associated quote mint token account.
/// 4. `[WRITE]` `base_market_ata` The market's associated base mint token account.
/// 5. `[WRITE]` `quote_market_ata` The market's associated quote mint token account.
/// 6. `[READ]` `base_mint` The base token mint account.
/// 7. `[READ]` `quote_mint` The quote token mint account.
/// 8. `[READ]` `base_token_program` The base mint's token program.
/// 9. `[READ]` `quote_token_program` The quote mint's token program.
pub struct CloseSeat<'a> {
    /// The user closing their seat.
    pub user: &'a pinocchio::accoun

Fixes #156 

